        "original_id": "888ae6e9-37ee-5a95-9acf-dba8e831e53c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Year Books\n- People: Edward I of England, Roger de Bankwell\n- Roles: King of England, counsel, justice of the King's Bench\n- Places: England, Gascony, Wales, Ireland, Scotland\n\nFacts:\n- The Year Books are the modern English name now typically given to the earliest law reports of England.\n- The Year Books survive in a continuous series from 1268 to 1535 and cover the reigns from Edward I to Henry VIII.\n- Edward I of England, also known as Edward Longshanks and the Hammer of the Scots, lived from 17 or 18 June 1239 to 7 July 1307 and was King of England from 1272 to 1307.\n- Roger de Bankwell appeared as counsel in the Year Book for 1340.\n- In 1341, Roger de Bankwell was appointed to a justiceship of the King's Bench.\n- Between 1341 and 1347, Roger de Bankwell was one of those assigned to try petitions from Gascony, Wales, Ireland, Scotland, and other foreign parts.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "8c3d4a29-a3b8-5346-bfd3-c6bff099e1e1",
        "text": "Page: Year Books. Sentence: The Year Books are the modern English name that is now typically given to the earliest law reports of England.\nPage: Year Books. Sentence: They are extant in a continuous series from 1268 to 1535, covering the reigns of King Edward I to Henry VIII.\nPage: Edward I of England. Sentence: Edward I (17/18 June 1239 – 7 July 1307), also known as Edward Longshanks and the Hammer of the Scots (Latin: \"Malleus Scotorum\" ), was King of England from 1272 to 1307.\nPage: Roger de Bankwell. Sentence: He appears as a counsel in the Year book for 1340, in 1341 was appointed to a justiceship of the king's bench, and was one of those assigned to try petitions from Gascony, Wales, Ireland, Scotland, and 'other foreign parts ' between the years 1341 and 1347.",
